<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:25:21Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-ger" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-1432?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Germany rejects Trump demand, 'fully supports' Zelenskyy</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:40082699e963fca54bd4f796bec91f20781360e99099c611508a3ee6cdd05550</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:25:16Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T09:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-rejects-trump-demand-fully-supports-zelenskyy/live-79627811--4ec6095d1e6c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-rejects-trump-demand-fully-supports-zelenskyy/live-79627811?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Germany's foreign minister has said Berlin is behind Ukraine's president and will keep pressuring Russia to end its war. Meanwhile, Deutsche Bank's CEO warned that political extremism is spooking investors. More on DW.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>German 'lawfare': When lawsuits become a political weapon</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:bb49495f1361a24382782994a0cb7b96fc405e00351b10699dffbd317dfe3305</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:25:06Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T15:14:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/german-lawfare-when-lawsuits-become-a-political-weapon/a-79615817--29d1d48e91a2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/german-lawfare-when-lawsuits-become-a-political-weapon/a-79615817?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The Left Party is suing two government ministers for accusing them of antisemitism, while Chancellor Friedrich Merz has been sued several times by various parties. Is German politics descending into "lawfare?"</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>Germany's population is shrinking, but not everywhere</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c0263b63a2f3e1b9d60c3233df1dfb1c30dda1969b2014a4b80679dd0916c582</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:25:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T07:21:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-population-is-shrinking-but-not-everywhere/a-79615807--3e9fde1b2ecd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-population-is-shrinking-but-not-everywhere/a-79615807?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">By 2045, Germany will have more older people, fewer workers and a smaller population overall. Yet some regions will defy this trend. Their success depends on how much they have to offer.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>'Ragebait' is the 2026 German Youth Word of the Year</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:224f082c59f6fe29f2c649ce513f9dada024dbce3912240b212e6fcb1bca78ac</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:24:06Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T13:36:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ragebait-is-the-2026-german-youth-word-of-the-year/a-79608158--c39c4c350c71.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ragebait-is-the-2026-german-youth-word-of-the-year/a-79608158?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Already named the 2025 Oxford Word of the Year, "rage bait" has also been widely adopted in German slang.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Servers at Munich's iconic Oktoberfest call for more respect</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9eb74ecf285f2b33fdc418ead1d33e89c82615450c63b0536f03f141569f9248</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:24:06Z</updated><published>2026-09-30T13:22:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/servers-at-munich-s-iconic-oktoberfest-call-for-more-respect/a-79491207--cd91ce591124.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/servers-at-munich-s-iconic-oktoberfest-call-for-more-respect/a-79491207?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Oktoberfest is famous the world over for beer, brass band music and Bavarian traditions. But staff are also reporting incidents of racism and sexism, and are taking a stand against discrimination.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Three-way battle for power in world chess: Who will be elected FIDE president in Samarkand?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:41465734b777a0c6b6cf12bfbbc18b0f3bd2fe4b340f6c4eab9c5fbe099e667e</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:24:06Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T18:59:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/three-way-battle-for-power-in-world-chess-who-will-be-elected-fide-president-in-samarkand/a-79413301--bc60333e85fc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/three-way-battle-for-power-in-world-chess-who-will-be-elected-fide-president-in-samarkand/a-79413301?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The era of Russian presidents is coming to an end at the World Chess Federation (FIDE). Two Germans and one Kazakh are up for election. Among the issues at play are money, Kremlin influence, and unanswered questions.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry></feed>
